            <ArticleTitle>The correlation between lymphocyte/monocyte ratio and coronary collateral circulation in stable coronary artery disease patients.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>43-52</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="AIM" NlmCategory="OBJECTIVE">Coronary collateral circulation (CCC) has an important impact on cardiovascular prognosis and well-developed CCC is associated with better clinical outcomes. We investigated whether lymphocyte/monocyte ratio (LMR) has an association with CCC in patients with stable coronary artery disease (SCAD).</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="METHODS" NlmCategory="METHODS">The study population consisted of 245 patients with SCAD. Patients were classified into a poor CCC group (Rentrop grades 0/1, n = 87), or good CCC group (Rentrop grades 2/3, n = 158).</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="RESULTS" NlmCategory="RESULTS">LMR values were significantly higher in patients with good CCC than in those with poor CCC (4.41 ± 1.58 vs 2.76 ± 1.10; p &lt; 0.001). In receiver operating characteristic analysis, optimal cutoff of LMR for predicting well-developed CCC was 3.38. In multivariate analysis, LMR &gt;3.38 (OR 4.637; p = 0.004), high sensitivity C-reactive protein (OR 0.810, p &lt; 0.001), dyslipidemia (OR 2.485; p = 0.039), and presence of chronic total occlusion (OR 16.836; p &lt; 0.001) were independent predictors of well-developed CCC.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="CONCLUSION" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">Increased LMR predicts well-developed CCC in SCAD patients.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
